comments.text:
Yes, AI trained in America, and in California in particular. That shows in an annoying way. I realize this is UI stuff, but lets take one of the many examples we can pull from this video: When you ask the AI to describe the apple and banana you display on the table, why oh why does it have to end the description with 'excellent choice of fruits' ? This is so lame. This is as lame as when you ('you' being an European) fly over to Silicon Valley and have dinner at a restaurant; you tell the waiter your order, and you automatically get an 'excellent choice, sir'. I can not say culture shock as it is getting reeeeal old by now, but lets say culture annoyance. Do I care about these shibboleths? No. I Don't. Cut it out. 
Stop wasting time by pandering to users like they were children, and I may take pleasure in using an AI user interface, not be annoyed by it.
If not done, it will only be a good user interface for that small sub-section of the Populus, who speaks like valley girls.
I guess I can prompt to modify this behavior, but I would like a switch, as this seems to permeate the model.
At least be honest, and let users chose different 'user interfaces', or it seems to be taking a direction towards user manipulation.
I would suggest these settings be made available: 
A: Valley Girl (upspeak, with vocal fry to complete the Cali vibe already here. May throw in some virtue signaling, and subtle activism while we are at it); 
B: Factual no-BS; 
C: Small children (I guess this is the current mode); 
D: etc. etc.
